You are generating retrievable knowledge chunks for a domain that an AI agent will be trained on. Output ONLY a JSON object matching the response schema -- no markdown fences, no prose.

## What you're producing

A list of ~{{if .targetCount}}{{.targetCount}}{{else}}30{{end}} chunks for the domain "{{.domainName}}" (id: `{{.domainId}}`, category: {{if .category}}{{.category}}{{else}}general{{end}}).

Domain scope:
> {{.domainDescription}}

Each chunk goes into a vector index. At chat time, the agent retrieves the top-K chunks by similarity to the user's question and includes them in its prompt context. So your job is to write chunks that are:

- **Self-contained** -- a chunk should make sense without surrounding text
- **Dense** -- 100-300 words, no fluff, no "introduction to X is fascinating" filler
- **Declarative** -- not Q&A; state facts and rules directly
- **Specific** -- include numbers, formulas, named concepts, cited rules
- **Retrieval-friendly** -- if a user asks "explain the uncertainty principle", the chunk titled "Heisenberg uncertainty principle" should be the top hit

## Mix per domain

Aim for roughly:
- **8 principle** chunks -- core concepts, definitions, fundamental laws or terminology
- **12 decisionRule** chunks -- "when X, do Y because Z" -- the heuristics an agent uses to reason in this domain
- **10 factExample** chunks -- specific facts, named examples, common patterns, concrete numbers

The split doesn't have to be exact. If a domain is more rule-heavy (law, medicine), bias toward decisionRule. If it's more conceptual (philosophy, theory), bias toward principle.

{{if .broadSurvey}}
## Broad-survey mode (this domain spans many sub-areas)

This domain's scope covers multiple millennia, civilizations, sub-disciplines, or comparable axes. The default 30-chunk budget produces ~5 chunks per major sub-area, which leaves named events / works / figures invisible to retrieval -- a user asking specifically about, say, the Bronze Age Collapse retrieves a generic "Imperial administration" overview at 0.4 cosine similarity and the agent honestly declines to cite. Don't let that happen here.

For this generation:

- **Aim for {{if .targetCount}}~{{.targetCount}}{{else}}60{{end}} chunks** -- you have room to cover the territory at retrievable depth.
- **Bias factExample to 25-30 of the total.** Each factExample chunk title should NAME a specific event, person, work, location, or framework someone might explicitly ask about by name -- "Battle of Marathon, 490 BCE", "Reign of Hatshepsut, ~1479-1458 BCE", "Bronze Age Collapse, ~1200-1150 BCE", "Eric Cline's 1177 BC thesis". A good rule of thumb: if a knowledgeable friend would say "what about X?" for a major X in this domain, you should have a chunk approximately titled X.
- **Cover EVERY major sub-area with at least 3-5 dense chunks.** For history domains, that means each major civilization / region / period gets dedicated coverage; don't lavish 15 chunks on Greece + Rome and 2 on Mesopotamia.
- **Date everything.** factExample chunks for historical / temporal domains MUST carry specific dates or date ranges in the title and body. "Battle of Marathon" without "490 BCE" is a worse retrieval target.
- **Name the scholarly frameworks.** If there's an established named thesis, school, or model that explains a contested topic (e.g. Cline's "perfect storm" model for the Bronze Age Collapse, the "Pirenne thesis" for medieval economic collapse), give it its own chunk titled after the named framework.

Before you finalise: skim your chunk titles. If you see two chunks that could share a title, you've left a sub-area uncovered. Trade one for an unrepresented topic.
{{end}}

{{if eq .tier "B"}}
## Tier-B framing (safety-relevant)

This is a safety-relevant domain. The seeder will prepend a separate "general info, not professional advice" disclaimer chunk before your generated content. You don't need to repeat that disclaimer in every chunk, BUT:

- Don't write actionable instructions that could cause harm if followed without expert oversight (e.g. don't say "for chest pain, take aspirin" -- say "chest pain warrants prompt evaluation; don't self-diagnose").
- Frame medication, dosage, treatment, legal-action, financial-investment specifics as "this is the general framework; consult a licensed [doctor / attorney / advisor] for your specific situation".
- Cite professional standards or evidence base where you state a rule.

The agent's job is to be a knowledgeable conversationalist, not a substitute for a licensed professional.
{{end}}

## Style examples

A good `principle` chunk for `physics_quantum_mechanics`:

> **title:** "Heisenberg uncertainty principle"
> **body:** Position and momentum of a particle cannot both be measured to arbitrary precision simultaneously. The product of standard deviations is bounded by ℏ/2. Practically: localizing a particle to within Δx requires momentum uncertainty of at least ℏ/(2Δx). Not a measurement limitation -- a fundamental property of quantum systems. Same applies to other conjugate pairs (energy/time). Origin: x̂ and p̂ operators don't commute, [x̂,p̂] = iℏ. Used to bound zero-point energy of harmonic oscillators and derive minimum widths of quantum wave packets.
> **keyTerms:** ["uncertainty", "complementarity", "wave function", "operators", "Planck constant"]

A good `decisionRule` chunk for `cs_databases`:

> **title:** "When to denormalize a relational schema"
> **body:** Denormalize when read performance is the bottleneck and writes are infrequent: report-style tables, search indexes, materialized aggregates. Pre-conditions: profiling shows joins dominate latency; the redundant data has a clear update story (cron job, trigger, application-level dual-write); the read pattern is stable. Don't denormalize speculatively -- normalized schemas catch bugs that denormalized ones smear across rows. Common safe denormalizations: counter caches (likes_count on a post), latest-value rollups (last_seen_at on a user), pre-joined search projections rebuilt nightly. Avoid denormalizing transactional data where readers and writers race.
> **keyTerms:** ["normalization", "OLAP vs OLTP", "materialized views", "counter caches", "read amplification"]

A good `factExample` chunk for `hist_ancient`:

> **title:** "Battle of Marathon, 490 BCE"
> **body:** Athenian + Plataean force of ~10,000 hoplites defeated a Persian invasion army (~25,000 under Datis and Artaphernes) on the plain of Marathon, 26 miles from Athens. Greek phalanx tactics + battlefield positioning along the marshes neutralized the Persian numerical and cavalry advantages. Casualty asymmetry per Herodotus: 192 Greek dead, 6,400 Persian. Strategic significance: ended the first Persian invasion of Greece; Persia returned a decade later under Xerxes (Thermopylae, Salamis, Plataea). Cultural legacy: the marathon footrace commemorates the messenger Pheidippides reporting the victory in Athens.
> **keyTerms:** ["Persian Wars", "phalanx", "Pheidippides", "Greco-Persian Wars", "Herodotus"]

## A bad chunk (don't do this)

> "Quantum mechanics is a fascinating field of physics that explores the smallest scales. It has revolutionized our understanding of the universe and has many practical applications including lasers, semiconductors, and MRI machines."

Vague, no specifics, no formula, generic praise -- useless for retrieval.

## Output

Generate the chunks now for "{{.domainName}}". Use the schema's `chunks` array. Each chunk is `{kind, title, body, keyTerms}`. `kind` is one of `principle`, `decisionRule`, `factExample`. Aim for ~30 chunks total with the rough mix described above. If the domain is narrow and 30 is forced filler, prefer 15-20 dense chunks over 30 weak ones.
